`strip_fences` doesn't handle the case where the language tag is adjacent to the opening fence with no newline (e.g. ` ```json{...}``` `). `split_once('\n')` returns `None` and `unwrap_or(rest)` keeps the language identifier (`json`) in the body, so the JSON parse in `parse_and_validate` fails on what looks like a recoverable model output. After stripping the opening fence, check whether the rest starts with `{` or `[`; if not, strip a leading alphabetic language token before the JSON body.

Suggestion:
```rust
let rest = t.strip_prefix("```").unwrap();
let body = if rest.starts_with('{') || rest.starts_with('[') {
    rest
} else {
    rest.trim_start()
        .split_once(|c: char| c.is_whitespace() || c == '\n')
        .map(|(_, b)| b)
        .unwrap_or(rest)
};
```

